{"id": "ch21", "number": "21", "title": "Derived rules", "titleInlines": [{"type": "text", "text": "Derived rules"}], "part": "IV", "blocks": [{"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "In this section, we will see why we introduced the rules of our proof system in two separate batches. In particular, we want to show that the additional rules of "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:Further", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch19", "text": "chapter 19"}, {"type": "text", "text": " are not strictly speaking necessary, but can be derived from the basic rules of "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:BasicTFL", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch17", "text": "chapter 17"}, {"type": "text", "text": "."}]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 2, "number": "21.1", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Derivation of Reiteration"}], "id": "sec-21-1"}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "To illustrate what it means to derive a "}, {"type": "emph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "rule"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " from other rules, first consider reiteration. It is a basic rule of our system, but it is also not necessary. Suppose you have some sentence on some line of your deduction:"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": null, "refs": null}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "You now want to repeat yourself, on some line "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "k", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ". You could just invoke the rule R. But equally well, you can do this with other basic rules of "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:BasicTFL", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch17", "text": "chapter 17"}, {"type": "text", "text": ":"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "k", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A} \\land \\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\land"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I"}], "refs": "m, m", "ruleText": "∧I"}, {"num": "k+1", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\land"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "k", "ruleText": "∧E"}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "To be clear: this is not a proof. Rather, it is a proof "}, {"type": "emph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "scheme"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ". After all, it uses a variable, ‘"}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\mathcal{A}", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "’, rather than a sentence of TFL, but the point is simple: Whatever sentences of TFL we plugged in for ‘"}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\mathcal{A}", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "’, and whatever lines we were working on, we could produce a bona fide proof. So you can think of this as a recipe for producing proofs."}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Indeed, it is a recipe which shows us the following: anything we can prove using the rule R, we can prove (with one more line) using just the basic rules of "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:BasicTFL", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch17", "text": "chapter 17"}, {"type": "text", "text": " without R. That is what it means to say that the rule R can be derived from the other basic rules: anything that can be justified using R can be justified using only the other basic rules."}]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 2, "number": "21.2", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Derivation of Disjunctive Syllogism"}], "id": "sec-21-2"}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Suppose that you are in a proof, and you have something of this form:"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}\\lor \\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "n", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": null, "refs": null}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "You now want, on line "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "k", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ", to prove "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\mathcal{B}", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ". You can do this with the rule of DS, introduced in "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:Further", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch19", "text": "chapter 19"}, {"type": "text", "text": ", but equally well, you can do this with the "}, {"type": "emph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "basic"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " rules of "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:BasicTFL", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch17", "text": "chapter 17"}, {"type": "text", "text": ":"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}\\lor \\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "n", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "k", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "k+1", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\bot", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "n, k", "ruleText": "¬E"}, {"num": "k+2", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "X"}], "refs": "k+1", "ruleText": "X"}, {"num": "k+3", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "k+4", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "R"}], "refs": "k+3", "ruleText": "R"}, {"num": "k+5", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lor"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "m, k–k+2, k+3–k+4", "ruleText": "∨E"}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "So the DS rule, again, can be derived from our more basic rules. Adding it to our system did not make any new proofs possible. Anytime you use the DS rule, you could always take a few extra lines and prove the same thing using only our basic rules. It is a "}, {"type": "emph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "derived"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " rule."}]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 2, "number": "21.3", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Derivation of Modus Tollens"}], "id": "sec-21-3"}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Suppose you have the following in your proof:"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}\\rightarrow \\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "n", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "You now want, on line "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "k", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ", to prove "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ". You can do this with the rule of MT, introduced in "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:Further", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch19", "text": "chapter 19"}, {"type": "text", "text": ". Equally well, you can do this with the "}, {"type": "emph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "basic"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " rules of "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:BasicTFL", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch17", "text": "chapter 17"}, {"type": "text", "text": ":"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}\\rightarrow \\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "n", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "k", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "k+1", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\rightarrow"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "m, k", "ruleText": "→E"}, {"num": "k+2", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\bot", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "n, k+1", "ruleText": "¬E"}, {"num": "k+3", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I"}], "refs": "k–k+2", "ruleText": "¬I"}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Again, the rule of MT can be derived from the "}, {"type": "emph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "basic"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " rules of "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:BasicTFL", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch17", "text": "chapter 17"}, {"type": "text", "text": "."}]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 2, "number": "21.4", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Derivation of Double-Negation Elimination"}], "id": "sec-21-4"}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Consider the following deduction scheme:"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "k", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "k+1", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\bot", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "m, k", "ruleText": "¬E"}, {"num": "k+2", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "IP"}], "refs": "k–k+1", "ruleText": "IP"}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Again, we can derive the DNE rule from the "}, {"type": "emph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "basic"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " rules of "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:BasicTFL", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch17", "text": "chapter 17"}, {"type": "text", "text": "."}]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 2, "number": "21.5", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Derivation of Excluded Middle"}], "id": "sec-21-5"}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Suppose you want to prove something using the LEM rule, i.e., you have in your proof"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "n", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "k", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "l", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "You now want, on line "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "l+1", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ", to prove "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\mathcal{B}", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ". The rule LEM from "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:Further", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch19", "text": "chapter 19"}, {"type": "text", "text": " would allow you to do it. But can you do this with the "}, {"type": "emph", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "basic"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " rules of "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:BasicTFL", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch17", "text": "chapter 17"}, {"type": "text", "text": "?"}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "One option is to first prove "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\mathcal{A} \\lor \\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ", and then apply "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\lor", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "E, i.e., proof by cases:"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "n", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "k", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "l", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\vdots", "rule": null, "refs": null, "ellipsis": true}, {"num": "i", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A} \\lor \\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "i+1", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lor"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "i, m–n, k–l", "ruleText": "∨E"}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "(We gave a proof of "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\mathcal{A} \\lor \\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": " using only our basic rules in "}, {"type": "ref", "label": "s:proofLEM", "variant": "cref", "href": "/read/ch18#sec-18-6", "text": "section 18.6"}, {"type": "text", "text": ".)"}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Here is another way that is a bit more complicated than the ones before. What you have to do is embed your two subproofs inside another subproof. The assumption of the subproof will be "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot \\mathcal{B}", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ", and the last line will be "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\bot", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ". Thus, the complete subproof is the kind you need to conclude "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\mathcal{B}"}, {"type": "text", "text": " using IP. Inside the proof, you’d have to do a bit more work to get "}, {"type": "math", "latex": "\\bot", "display": false}, {"type": "text", "text": ":"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "m+1", "depth": 2, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "", "depth": 2, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\vdots", "rule": null, "refs": null, "ellipsis": true}, {"num": "n", "depth": 2, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "n+1", "depth": 2, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\bot", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "m, n", "ruleText": "¬E"}, {"num": "n+2", "depth": 2, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "", "depth": 2, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\vdots", "rule": null, "refs": null, "ellipsis": true}, {"num": "l", "depth": 2, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "l+1", "depth": 2, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\bot", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "m, l", "ruleText": "¬E"}, {"num": "l+2", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I"}], "refs": "(m+1)–(n+1)", "ruleText": "¬I"}, {"num": "l+3", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I"}], "refs": "(n+2)–(l+1)", "ruleText": "¬I"}, {"num": "l+4", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\bot", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "l+3, l+2", "ruleText": "¬E"}, {"num": "l+5", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "IP"}], "refs": "m–(l+4)", "ruleText": "IP"}]}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Note that because we add an assumption at the top and additional conclusions inside the subproofs, the line numbers change. You may have to stare at this for a while before you understand what’s going on."}]}, {"type": "heading", "level": 2, "number": "21.6", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Derivation of De Morgan rules"}], "id": "sec-21-6"}, {"type": "para", "children": [{"type": "text", "text": "Here is a demonstration of how we could derive the first De Morgan rule:"}]}, {"type": "fitch", "lines": [{"num": "m", "depth": 0, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot (\\mathcal{A} \\land \\mathcal{B})", "rule": null, "refs": null}, {"num": "k", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "k+1", "depth": 2, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "k+2", "depth": 2, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\mathcal{A} \\land \\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\land"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I"}], "refs": "k, k+1", "ruleText": "∧I"}, {"num": "k+3", "depth": 2, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\bot", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "E"}], "refs": "m, k+2", "ruleText": "¬E"}, {"num": "k+4", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lnot"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I"}], "refs": "(k+1)–(k+3)", "ruleText": "¬I"}, {"num": "k+5", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A} \\lor \\lnot \\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lor"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I"}], "refs": "k+4", "ruleText": "∨I"}, {"num": "k+6", "depth": 1, "hypo": true, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A}", "rule": [{"type": "text", "text": "AS"}], "refs": "", "ruleText": "AS"}, {"num": "k+7", "depth": 1, "hypo": false, "formula": "\\lnot \\mathcal{A} \\lor \\lnot \\mathcal{B}", "rule": [{"type": "math", "latex": "\\lo